commons bynk.range {
	---
	Whether a `Range` request may be answered with the part (`PartialContent`) rather than the
	whole body, given the request's `If-Range` header and the entity tag of the representation
	as it is now (RFC 9110 §13.1.5).

	No `If-Range` (`None`) means the range stands. An `If-Range` entity tag holds only when it is
	exactly `etag` and neither is weak (`W/"…"`), the strong comparison `If-Range` requires. An
	`If-Range` that is a date never holds here, so the handler answers the whole body, which is
	always correct; compare a date with the object's `Last-Modified` yourself to honour it.

	When this is `false`, answer `200` with the full body (`RawStreamed`), not a `206`. The router
	refuses to send a `PartialContent` whose validator does not match the request's `If-Range`, so
	a handler that skips this check faults (`500`) instead of sending stale bytes.
	---
	fn ifRangeHolds(ifRange: Option[String], etag: String) -> Bool {
		match ifRange {
			None => true
			Some(held) => held == etag && !etag.startsWith("W/")
		}
	}
}
